(vector
  (map
    (:adr/id "adr-261021-native-library-compat")
    (:adr/title "Qualify native standard library readings without changing host APIs")
    (:adr/status "accepted")
    (:adr/date "2026-10-21")
    (:adr/authority "Kotoba owner request JUN-203")
    (:adr/body "Package IDs and exact import aliases retain their existing Kotoba naming contract. A package can contain host and native readings; the catalog ID selects source, and does not grant effects or promise that every host operation has a native counterpart. Amu, Kototama and other execution or authority owners remain independent repositories.

Native I/O is an immutable cursor and buffer API. A buffer is bytes, a reader yields one buffer chunk and an explicit eof?/advance cursor, and write!/copy return the updated writer. written obtains its bytes. Native code must thread returned state; host protocol implementations and their mutation behavior stay unchanged. Closed records use internal octet vectors supported by the pinned schema/compiler. Native does not synthesize a host protocol or an OS endpoint.

JSON decode and data-json read-str return [:result :document :document] on native. EDN read-document/read-all return the same result type, and write-string returns [:result :string :document]. Typed parser errors are caught inside the exported function. Native has UTF-8 byte offsets and valid UTF-8 strings. The host JSON/EDN return values, exception APIs and aliases remain. Invalid JSON trailing commas, non-string object keys and unescaped control characters are refused by every reading. Native EDN is a bounded subset: it refuses tagged literals, namespaced maps, character literals and host-specific numbers. The language-owned document reader can still trap on malformed builtin-parser input such as duplicate map keys; a typed result does not catch VM traps or resource exhaustion. These cases remain fail-closed, never a host fallback.

Native process exposes pure validate-spawn only, using UTF-8 byte limits. It cannot spawn an OS process. Host process handles, exec and sh retain their APIs. Native text exposes the required starts-with?, includes?, Unicode blank? and ASCII-only ascii-lower operations; host literal string separators and ASCII folding are available through the existing aliases. Existing Unicode case APIs are not silently replaced with ASCII behavior.

Native async exposes channel-take because the current language reserves take; existing host take remains. Bounded channel and structured scope state machines are compiled and run with the current compiler graph and the pinned self-built compiler. Historical compiler-only results cannot stand in for current compatibility.

An adapted branch port retains immutable input snapshots and hashes beside separately hashed integrated files. New native facades require explicit source-addition records. Retirement requires reviewed branch intent, host regression coverage, native source closure build/link/execution, successful merged-main CI, public import/CLI checks and fresh source-repository state readback. History, pins, branches, stashes and WIP are preserved. Independent bot profile/cron sources retain their identities and authority boundaries outside the library import paths. Their source home may move to the monorepo root only with a complete byte-bound copy and a matching canonical agent-registry migration; host materialization and scheduler activation are separate operations. Reviewed WIP keeps its original bytes and parent snapshot beside corrected integrated outputs before its source repository can retire.")))
